7. Leave was granted to consider the following submissions made by the learned Additional Attorney General The suit land being reserve forest under Notification 3125-FOR dated id April 1955 issued under Section 4 of the Forest Act, 1927 (EXt.1) Tide Suit No. 160 of 1966 was barred under Section 20(2)(a) (iii) of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 and as such the trial Court had no jurisdiction to try that suit in view of the provision of Section 9 of the Code of Civil Procedure  and hence the impugned exparte decree passed in the aforesaid title suit being void, abilities ought to have been set aside by the High Court Division; 

 8. Mr. A. W. Bhuiyan, learned Additional Attorney General appearing for the appellant, submits that in the plaint of Title Suit No. 160 of 1966 the plaintiff admitted that the Suitland has been recorded as government forest and that  the present appellant has proved Ens. the gazette notification in support of the nature of the suit land as the recorded government forest and as such in view of the provision of sub-section (2)(a)(iii) of section 20 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 the learned Judge of the High Court Division erred in not holding that the Title Suit No. 160 of 1966 was i not maintainable and thereby the exparte decree dated 9.9.67 passed in that suit being a nullity is liable to be set aside. Mr. Bhuiyan, further submits that the plaintiffs having proved that they were not informed about the date of hearing of Title Suit No. 160 of 1966 and were also not informed about the requirement to pay the adjournment cost and as a result the plaintiff the could neither pay adjournment costs nor could contest the suit on the date of its peremptory hearing and thereby the plaintiff legally proved the necessary facts and circumstances to substantiate the allegations of collusion in between the clerk of the Government pleader and the  plaintiff of the Title Suit No. 160 of 966, the lower appellate court upon improper assumption of facts wrongly revered the finding of collusion, found by the trial Court on the evidence on record and as such the learned Judge of the High Court Division erred in not setting aside  the judgment of the lower appellate court at least in the greater interest of doing justice. 

 9. Syed Badrul Alam, learned Advocate appearing for defendant respondent No. 1, on the other hand, submits that Abbas Ali Munshi got the settlement of the suit land from the Ex-Zaminder as far back as in 1946 and as such it cannot be the said that the learned Subordinate Judge had no jurisdiction to try Title Suit No. 160 of 1966 in view of the provision of Section 20(2) (a) (iii) of State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950.
